# Floral Depth Architecture — Material-Agnostic Theory and Permanent Gates

**Status:** computational design governance only  
**Sensory, realism, liking, performance, stability, safety, and release:** `NOT TESTED`  
**Scope:** all floral-primary perfumes, including soliflores, bouquets, floral-dominant families, and flower-led hybrids

## Universal doctrine and precedence

The universal depth doctrine at
[docs/depth_architecture/README.md](depth_architecture/README.md) has normative precedence
for construct boundaries, cross-family translation, non-scalar
comparison, experimental validation, and claim ceilings. This floral document
is the stricter family adapter: it adds flower-specific anatomy, realism,
coupling, wood-echo, and anti-collapse requirements, but it cannot redefine
ingredient count as perceptual depth or promote computational design to sensory
evidence. If an abbreviated floral rule conflicts with the universal volumes,
follow the universal doctrine and then apply the stricter target-specific
floral constraint.

## 1. Why the first White Fire design failed

The first White Fire Reliquary design compressed four flower anatomies and an
explicit DHP-scale depth ambition into 26 total rows and three principal wood
rows. The error was not simply “too few ingredients.” It was a failure of the
compiler contract:

1. The anti-padding rule was incorrectly used as a stopping rule.
2. Named “planes” stood in for independently resolved functions.
3. The benchmark was prose, not an executable resolution commitment.
4. Tests accepted two floral facets, one coupling, and a two-material wood
   dyad as sufficient for a four-flower depth competitor.
5. OAV and temporal screens constrained dose but could not detect missing
   flower anatomy, interfaces, or post-ablation collapse.

The correction is deliberately two-sided:

- material count, percentage, darkness, persistence, or computed OAV can never
  establish depth; and
- a target that explicitly requires high resolution may not silently compress
  away declared roles, interfaces, temporal registers, or causal controls.

The program now represents those commitments with
`FloralArchitectureContractV1`, `FloralSubjectAnatomyV1`,
`FloralMaterialRoleV1`, and `FloralInterfaceEdgeV1` in
`engine/perception/floral_depth.py`.

## 2. Scientific boundary

Odor mixtures are configurally processed, relative salience controls which
components remain recognizable, and small ratio changes can change the
resulting quality. Humans also have limited component-identification capacity.
Therefore, “more rows” can produce suppression, blur, or an emergent generic
object rather than more depth. Equal-intensity, broadly distributed mixtures
of about 30 components can converge toward “olfactory white”; this is an
anti-padding warning, not a 30-row perfume limit.

OAV is a screening instrument. It can identify a modeled threshold problem,
stock-strength error, or gross dominance risk. It does not predict mixture
pleasantness, named-flower realism, texture, spatial depth, or successful
temporal transformation. Molecular-complexity research on single molecules
must not be converted into a formula-row-count theory.

The only admissible path from design to sensory claims is controlled physical
evidence: recombination, omission, perturbation, anti-collapse, temporal, and
blinded preference work with the target identity held explicit.

## 3. Universal depth grammar across material classes

Every retained material receives one primary, independently falsifiable role.
A material may participate in more than one interface, but duplicated labels
do not create new resolution.

| Material class | Target-linked information it may carry | Useful resolution axes | Typical collapse risk | Required causal check |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Floral naturals and recognizers | named flower identity and stage | petal, flesh, pollen, nectar, wax, spice, fruit, shadow | generic white-floral mass or one-natural takeover | subject omission plus recognizer-retention endpoint |
| Floral modifiers | anatomy deformation or enrichment | cool/warm, dry/humid, translucent/fleshy, clean/animalic | decorative sweetness or “floralizer” blur | constant-total modifier ablation |
| Green, stem, leaf, humidity | living context and relief | crushed stem, leaf breath, dew, aqueous pressure, soil contact | shampoo, melon, cucumber, marine drift | low/target/high ratio perturbation |
| Citrus, aldehydic, aromatic top | entry vector and contrast | flash, rind, air, sparkle, bitter branch, aromatic lift | detached cologne top or adaptation hole | opening ablation and 0–30 minute temporal check |
| Woods | grain, enclosure, tactile counterpoint, recurrence | creamy/dry, rough/polished, root/mineral, transparent/dense, warm/cool | generic woody-amber wall or separate wood perfume | class ablation, texture perturbation, flower-retention endpoint |
| Resins, smoke, leather, animalic | shadow, patina, ritual heat, bodily edge | luminous/dark, clean/dirty, mineral/balsamic, suede/char | amber outsourcing, tar, medicinal or syrup drift | trace ladder and identity-preserving omission |
| Musks | skin continuity, diffusion contour, tactile finish | macrocyclic skin, clean lift, powder, fatty warmth | laundry fog, anosmic false absence, redundant fixation | zero/one/pairwise isolated test; genotype/anosmia caveat |
| Gourmand and sweet materials | nectar, pollen, ripeness, comfort | honeyed/dry, creamy/waxy, edible/non-edible | syrup, pastry, vanilla base replacing the flower | constant-sweetness substitution and target-drift endpoint |
| Carriers and fixatives | delivery and release geometry | partitioning, evaporation, film, application mass | mistaken “depth” from persistence alone | matched carrier/application and release-only analysis |

This grammar is material-agnostic. It applies to chemicals, EOs, and absolutes;
naturals remain whole formula identities even when decomposed for composite-OAV
diagnostics.

## 4. Target-first compilation procedure

1. Freeze the named identity, emotional tone, floral scope, realism target,
   forbidden drift, and claim ceiling.
2. Keep `TARGET / IDEAL` and `CURRENT-INVENTORY BUILD` as separate immutable
   ledgers, even when numerically identical.
3. For every lead and co-lead, declare PETAL and FLESH plus at least two
   target-specific anatomy facets. Add pollen, nectar, stem/leaf, humidity,
   wax, shadow, fruit, spice, or temporal transition only when the flower needs
   them.
4. Define recognizers by temporal window: opening, 5 minutes, 30 minutes,
   2 hours, 4 hours, and 8 hours. Later windows may transform identity but may
   not become an unrelated generic base by declaration.
5. Enumerate modules and interfaces before selecting doses. Every floral
   subject must touch an explicit interface; a flower-to-wood edge must name
   bridge materials, its failure mode, and a controlled trial.
6. Predeclare target-required primary roles. High-resolution benchmarks may
   enumerate many roles, but each must bind to one current-build material and
   a material-containing ablation trial. Missing roles cause HOLD because
   target functions disappeared—not because a universal row minimum exists.
7. Resolve wood/base texture and time independently from floral depth. Wood may
   amplify floral hedonism, recurrence, and enclosure; it may not be declared
   the flower's sole depth mechanism.
8. Choose zero or one exact musk by default. More than one musk requires a
   complete isolated interaction design. Specific anosmia remains a physical
   uncertainty.
9. Run the missing-chemical impact gate. A missing exact recognizer cannot be
   silently replaced by an inventory-near material.
10. Dose only after architecture closure. Any source-stock transfer below
    10 µL requires a practical prepared dilution with source transfer,
    carrier, final fraction, delivered volume, and active dose all explicit.
11. Run computational OAV and temporal diagnostics, then revise no more than
    two bounded times. A third structural failure triggers a clean rebuild.
12. Admit no sensory, performance, or release claim without physical evidence.

## 5. Permanent noncompensatory gates

These gates apply to every floral-primary design. Passing one cannot compensate
for failing another.

| Gate | Required evidence | HOLD condition |
|---|---|---|
| F0 Identity lock | named target, tone, subjects, realism, forbidden drift | target is generic or changes during optimization |
| F1 Ledger separation | distinct ideal and current-build references | target and inventory build collapse into one authority |
| F2 Source policy | EO, absolute, or known chemical when this policy is selected | opaque base, fragrance oil, FTEC, or unknown blend |
| F3 Subject anatomy | PETAL + FLESH + two target-specific facets for each lead/co-lead | any named flower is represented only by a label or generic base |
| F4 Role coverage | one unique primary role for every current row | unmapped row, duplicate primary role, or declared role compressed out |
| F5 Interface closure | module edge, bridge material, failure mode, controlled trial | rhetorical “bridge” or detached flower/wood planes |
| F6 Floral-depth independence | internal flower relations plus wood linkage | wood, amber, sweetness, or musk is the only stated depth source |
| F7 Temporal resolution | declared floral and wood/base bands | opening-only recognizer or generic late base by design |
| F8 Texture resolution | target-declared tactile axes bound to materials | “creamy,” “mineral,” or “polished” exists only as prose |
| F9 Effective complexity | ablation, perturbation, anti-collapse controls | no test can distinguish contribution from padding or genericization |
| F10 Musk exactness | zero/one musk or complete isolated multi-musk trial | redundant musk chord, exception musk without exact justification |
| F11 Measurement geometry | arithmetic closure and ≥10 µL source transfers | direct sub-10 µL dose or unbound serial dilution |
| F12 Claim ceiling | computational output labelled as design evidence | inferred liking, realism, similarity, safety, stability, or release |

### OAV integrity sub-gates

1. Verify exact stock strength before calculating active dose. A 10%-to-neat
   rebase at unchanged raw µL must change active dose and OAV; mutation tests
   permanently guard the historical Lemonile-type failure.
2. Resolve duplicate ODT keys and units; the active lookup value must be
   explicit.
3. Use composite-natural OAV for EOs, absolutes, and other natural mixtures.
   Never recompute their whole odor from bulk vapor pressure divided by one
   monomolecular threshold.
4. Preserve a known canonical direct OAV. Cross-check monomolecular direct OAV
   against vapor/ODT evidence; mismatches are invalid, not silently averaged.
5. Require exact agreement across formula state, evidence gate, release gate,
   compact report, and full rendered analysis. Unknown OAV must remain
   `UNKNOWN`, never zero.
6. Flag a material below OAV 1 when its declared role requires perceptibility.
   Structural/fixative rows may be sub-threshold only when their non-perceptual
   function is explicit.
7. Inspect OAV by time window and run dominance/cliff checks. Computed
   headspace remains a model and cannot establish physical projection,
   texture, depth, or liking.

## 6. Controlled evaluation ladder

Use constant-total, blinded, order-balanced microtrials. Replace removed raw
volume with the same carrier and, when needed, match active mass separately.

1. **Recognizer screen:** each named flower against its carrier-matched
   omission.
2. **Anatomy screen:** complete subject versus one-facet ablations.
3. **Interface screen:** full bridge versus carrier-matched bridge ablation.
4. **Wood texture screen:** elemental arms, selected mixtures, and ±10/20/30%
   perturbations with floral core fixed.
5. **Anti-collapse screen:** full architecture versus module ablations; record
   target identity, flower hierarchy, genericization, texture, temporal
   continuity, and liking separately.
6. **Temporal screen:** TCATA/TDS-style attributes plus targeted intensity at
   0, 5, and 30 minutes and 2, 4, 8, and 24 hours when practical.
7. **Reference screen:** authentic benchmarks may anchor depth, texture, and
   performance geometry only; they do not establish similarity or flower
   realism.

## 7. Primary literature anchors

- Jinks AG, Laing DG. *The analysis of odor mixtures by humans: evidence for a
  configurational process.* Physiology & Behavior 72 (2001), 51–63.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0031-9384(00)00407-8
- Le Berre E et al. *Just noticeable differences in component concentrations
  modify the odor quality of a blending mixture.* Chemical Senses 33 (2008),
  389–395. https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjn006
- Frank ME, Fletcher DB, Hettinger TP. *Recognition of the Component Odors in
  Mixtures.* Chemical Senses 42 (2017), 537–546.
  https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjx031
- Weiss T et al. *Perceptual convergence of multi-component mixtures in
  olfaction implies an olfactory white.* PNAS 109 (2012), 19959–19964.
  https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1208110109
- Lapid H, Harel D, Sobel N. *Prediction Models for the Pleasantness of Binary
  Mixtures in Olfaction.* Chemical Senses 33 (2008), 599–609.
  https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjn026
- Kermen F et al. *Molecular complexity determines the number of olfactory
  notes and the pleasantness of smells.* Scientific Reports 1 (2011), 206.
  https://doi.org/10.1038/srep00206
- Shang C et al. *Rapid determination of volatile constituents of Michelia
  alba flowers by GC-MS with SPME.* Journal of Chromatography A 942 (2002),
  283–288. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0021-9673(01)01382-6
- Rout PK et al. *The chemical composition of the flower oil and leaf oil of
  Michelia alba D.C.* Journal of Essential Oil Research 4 (1992), 5–11.
  https://doi.org/10.1080/10412905.1992.9698004
- Pineau N et al. *Temporal Dominance of Sensations: construction of the TDS
  curves and comparison with time-intensity.* Food Quality and Preference 20
  (2009), 450–455. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foodqual.2009.04.005
- Almeida RN et al. *Evaporation and permeation of fragrance applied to the
  skin.* Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research 58 (2019), 9644–9650.
  https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.iecr.9b01004
- Stappen I et al. *Structure-Activity Relationships of Sandalwood Odorants.*
  Natural Product Communications 5 (2010).
  https://doi.org/10.1177/1934578X1000500902
- Trimmer C et al. *Genetic variation in OR5AN1 associates with perception of
  musks.* Chemical Senses (2023). https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9874024/

These sources constrain the experimental logic. They do not authenticate a
commercial formula, prove the user's White Champi lot, or authorize a sensory
or safety conclusion.
